  "dom_id": "step-q136400",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A particle moves with a constant acceleration of $(2i-4j)\\,{\\text{ft/s}}^{2}.$ The velocity of the particle is $(-4i+16j)\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$ Find the time when the velocity of the particle equals $(8i-8j)\\,\\text{ft/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\na=2i-4j,\\,u=-4i+16j,\\,v=8i-8j\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 8i-8j & =(-4i+16j)+(2i-4j)t \\\\ 12i-24j & =(2i-4j)t \\\\ 12i-24j & =2ti-4tj\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the $i$ and $j$ components in the last equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12 & = & 2t \\\\ -24 & = & -4t\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,t=6\\,\\text{s}.\n$$",
